.th RATFOR 1
.sh NAME
ratfor \- rational Fortran dialect
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd ratfor
[ option ... ]
[ filename ... ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Ratfor
converts a rational dialect of Fortran into ordinary irrational Fortran.
.it Ratfor
provides control flow constructs essentially identical to those in C:
.sp 1
.in +7
.ti -7
statement grouping:
.nf
{ statement; statement; statement }
.sp 1
.ti -7
decision-making:
if (condition) statement [ else statement ]
.br
switch (integer value) {
	case integer:	statement
	...
	[ default: ]	statement
}
.sp 1
.ti -7
loops:
while (condition) statement
for (expression; condition; expression) statement
do limits statement
repeat statement [ until (condition) ]
break [n]
next [n]
.sp 1
.ti -7
and some syntactic sugar to make programs easier to read and write:
.sp 1
.ti -7
free form input:
multiple statements/line; automatic continuation
.sp 1
.ti -7
comments:
# this is a comment
.sp 1
.ti -7
translation of relationals:
>, >=, etc., become .GT., .GE., etc.
.sp 1
.ti -7
return (expression)
returns expression to caller from function
.sp 1
.ti -7
define:
define name replacement
.sp 1
.ti -7
include:
include filename
.sp 1
.in -7
.fi
The option
.bd \-h
causes quoted strings to be turned into
27H constructs.
.bd \-C
copies comments to the output, and attempts
to format it neatly.
Normally, continuation lines are marked with a &
in column 1;
the option
.bd \-6x
makes the continuation character
.bd x
and places it in column 6.
.sp 1
.it Ratfor
is best used with
.it f77 (1).
.sh "SEE ALSO"
f77(1)
.br
B. W. Kernighan and P. J. Plauger,
.it "Software Tools" ,
Addison-Wesley, 1976.
